\chapter{AST-Derived Visitors}
\label{ch:visitors}

A tree needs a way to be traversed, and a tagged tree needs a way to be
traversed \emph{according to its tags}. A simple recursive walk that
visits every node is easy to write and rarely what a language
implementer wants, because almost every interesting operation on a
syntax tree is a different operation per node kind: a type checker
computes a type for an expression, an evaluator computes a value, a
formatter prints, a constant folder folds. Each of these is a function
from a node to a result, and each of them must decide, for every node it
meets, which of its several behaviours applies.

That decision is a dispatch, and the classic way to express it in C++ is
the \code{std::variant} plus a chain of \code{std::visit} overloads, or,
when the tags are an enumeration, a \code{switch}. Both are effective and
both are closed: the set of node kinds is fixed at the point where the
dispatch is written, and adding a new kind means editing every dispatch
site in the program. For a language that is still being designed, that
coupling is a real cost.

DSLtk's answer is to make the visitor a \emph{derived} object. A user
supplies a schema---a description of which tags exist---and a set of
cases, one per tag, and the library builds a visitor that dispatches on
the tag. The schema may be given at run time, as a prototype
\code{ASTNode}, or at compile time, as a type, in which case the
exhaustiveness of the case set is checked by the compiler rather than at
run time. The same visitor, the same traversal, and the same
\code{walk} function serve both.

\section{Data model}

The vocabulary is four types and three functions.

\code{ASTTag} is the smallest of them: a wrapper around a compile-time
tag that exposes it as a \code{std::string_view}
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9963).

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The tag wrapper and the case holder.,label=lst:visitor-tag-case]
template <FixedString Tag> struct ASTTag
{
  static constexpr auto value = Tag.view ();
};

template <FixedString Tag, class F> struct VisitorCase
{
  static constexpr auto tag = Tag.view ();
  F fn;
  template <class Node>
  void
  invoke (Node &node)
  {
    if constexpr (std::invocable<F &, ASTTag<Tag>, Node &>)
      std::invoke (fn, ASTTag<Tag>{}, node);
    else
      std::invoke (fn, node);
  }
};
\end{lstlisting}

\code{VisitorCase} holds the handler and knows how to call it. The
\code{if constexpr} is the whole of the handler-shape flexibility: if the
handler can be called with a compile-time tag as well as the node, it is
called that way, and otherwise it is called with the node alone. A handler
that wants to know which tag it is handling may therefore write

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Two handler shapes, as they appear inside a function that builds a visitor.},label=lst:visitor-handlers]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>

void
build (dsl::ASTNode &tree)
{
  auto visitor = dsl::compile_visitor (
      tree, dsl::on<"num"> ([] (dsl::ASTNode &n) { std::cout << n.value () << "\n"; }),
      dsl::on<"add"> ([] (dsl::ASTTag<"add"> tag, dsl::ASTNode &) { std::cout << tag.value << "\n"; }));
  visitor.visit (tree);
}

int
main ()
{
  dsl::ASTNode tree ("add", { dsl::ASTNode ("num", "1") });
  build (tree);
}
\end{lstlisting}

and the second form is particularly useful when a single generic handler
is registered for several tags, since the tag is then available as a
compile-time constant.

The factory that produces a case is \api{on}. There is no
\code{visitor_case}: the tag is a template argument of \code{on}, and
\code{on<Tag>(fn)} returns a \code{VisitorCase<Tag, std::decay_t<F>>}
holding the handler. The spelling is short because the case is named far
more often than it is constructed, and because a reader who sees
\code{on<"num">} in a list of handlers understands immediately that the
list is a dispatch table.

\code{on} decays the handler type, so a lambda, a function pointer, and
a stateful functor are all accepted, and each keeps its own type in the
resulting tuple. That matters because the tuple's element types are
exactly what the dispatch compares: \code{VisitorCase::tag} is a
\code{std::string_view} derived from the template argument, so the tag
of a case is available without instantiating the handler at all.

\code{VisitOrder} is a two-valued enumeration, \code{Preorder} and
\code{Postorder}, that says whether a node is visited before or after its
children. \code{ASTVisitor} is the visitor itself: a tuple of cases plus
the list of tags the schema uses. The listing is an abridged excerpt: the
dispatch and traversal members follow the constructor in the header.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The visitor and its runtime factory.,label=lst:visitor-class]
template <class... Cases> class ASTVisitor
{
  std::tuple<Cases...> cases_;
  std::vector<std::string> tags_;
  void
  collect (const ASTNode &node)
  {
    if (std::find (tags_.begin (), tags_.end (), node.tag ()) == tags_.end ())
      tags_.emplace_back (node.tag ());
    for (const auto &child : node.children ())
      collect (child);
  }

public:
  ASTVisitor (const ASTNode &schema, Cases... cases)
      : cases_ (std::move (cases)...)
  {
    collect (schema);
    for (const auto &tag : tags_)
      if (!((tag == Cases::tag) || ...))
        throw std::invalid_argument ("visitor lacks a handler for " + tag);
  }
  // ... visit and walk
};
\end{lstlisting}

The constructor does two things. It walks the schema and collects the
distinct tags it contains, in the order of first occurrence in a
depth-first traversal. It then checks that every collected tag has a
corresponding case, and throws \code{std::invalid_argument} whose message is
\emph{visitor lacks a handler for}, followed by the offending tag if one does not. The error message names
the offending tag, which is usually enough to identify the missing
handler immediately.

