\section{Dispatch and traversal}

\code{visit} is the dispatch. It first checks that the node's tag is one
the schema knows, and throws \code{std::invalid_argument} whose message is
\emph{node tag is outside visitor schema} if it is not. It then applies a fold over the
tuple of cases and invokes the \emph{first} case whose tag matches the
node's tag. Exactly one case runs; the \code{invoked} flag guarantees it
even if two cases carried the same tag, in which case the one registered
first wins.

\code{walk} is the traversal. In preorder it visits the node and then
recurses into the children; in postorder it recurses first and visits
afterwards. The default is preorder. There is no \code{visit_all}: the
name would be redundant next to \code{walk}, and the library has only one
traversal.

\begin{pseudocode}[Dispatch on tag]
\textbf{function} visit (visitor, node)\\
\quad if the node's tag is not among the schema's tags then\\
\qquad raise \emph{node tag is outside visitor schema}\\
\quad for each case in registration order do\\
\qquad\qquad if the case's tag equals the node's tag then\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad invoke the case with the node\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad return
\end{pseudocode}

