\section{Two kinds of schema}

The runtime schema is a prototype \code{ASTNode}. It is a tree whose
shape does not matter---only its set of tags does---and the natural way
to build one is with the \code{node<>} and \code{leaf<>} factories of
\chapref{ch:leaf-and-node}. The visitor's constructor walks the prototype
and collects the tags, so the prototype's arities and values are
irrelevant. A prototype that says \code{(add (num 0))} is enough to
declare that the language has the tags \code{add} and \code{num}, even
though every real \code{add} node in the language has two children.

The compile-time schema is a type. \api{ASTSchema} records a tag and a
tuple of child schema types, and \api{ast_schema} builds one:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The compile-time schema type and its factory.,label=lst:ast-schema]
template <FixedString Tag, class... Children> struct ASTSchema
{
  static constexpr auto tag = Tag.view ();
  using children = std::tuple<Children...>;
};

template <FixedString Tag, class... Children>
constexpr auto
ast_schema (Children...)
{
  return ASTSchema<Tag, Children...>{};
}
\end{lstlisting}

There is no \code{schema} function; the factory is called
\code{ast_schema}, with the tag as its first template argument.

The compile-time schema carries strictly more information than the
runtime one, because it knows the arity of every node as well as its
tag. That extra information is what makes the compile-time check
possible. The predicate \api{visitor_detail::covered} is a
\code{constexpr bool} function that is true if and only if the schema's
own tag has a case and every child schema is recursively covered
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 10075):

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The compile-time coverage predicate.,label=lst:covered]
template <class Schema, class... Cases>
constexpr bool
covered ()
{
  constexpr bool own = ((Schema::tag == Cases::tag) || ...);
  return own &&
         []<std::size_t... I> (std::index_sequence<I...>)
           {
             return (
                 covered<std::tuple_element_t<I, typename Schema::children>,
                         Cases...> ()
                 && ...);
           }(std::make_index_sequence<
               std::tuple_size_v<typename Schema::children>>{});
}
\end{lstlisting}

Because it is a \code{constexpr} function, it can be used in a
\code{static_assert} by a user, and the library uses it in exactly that
way. \api{compile_visitor} has two overloads. The first takes a schema
\emph{type}, performs the check, and then derives the runtime visitor
from a materialised tree:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The two overloads of \code{compile_visitor}.},label=lst:compile-visitor]
template <class Schema, class... Cases>
auto
compile_visitor (Schema, Cases &&...cases)
{
  static_assert (visitor_detail::covered<Schema, std::decay_t<Cases>...> (),
                 "visitor must handle every schema tag");
  return derive_visitor (visitor_detail::tree<Schema> (),
                         std::forward<Cases> (cases)...);
}

template <class... Cases>
auto
compile_visitor (const ASTNode &schema, Cases &&...cases)
{
  return derive_visitor (schema, std::forward<Cases> (cases)...);
}
\end{lstlisting}

The second overload takes a runtime prototype and performs no compile-time
check; the coverage requirement is then enforced at run time by the
visitor's constructor, which throws.

The helper \api{visitor_detail::tree} materialises a runtime
\code{ASTNode} from a compile-time schema, recursively. It exists so that
the schema-typed overload of \code{compile_visitor} can reuse the
runtime visitor's tag-collection machinery instead of duplicating it. The
materialised tree is a prototype: it is never visited.

\subsection{Choosing between them}

The runtime prototype is the right choice when the schema is not known
until run time---when it is loaded from a file, when it is the result of
a previous parse, or when the same visitor code must serve several
languages. It costs nothing beyond a run-time check, and the check is
made once, when the visitor is constructed.

The compile-time schema is the right choice when the language is fixed
and the exhaustiveness of the visitor is a correctness property of the
program. It turns a missing handler from a run-time exception into a
compilation error, which is the difference between a bug found in
testing and a bug found before the program runs. It also makes the
missing-handler failure impossible to reach at run time at all, because
the program does not compile.

The cost of the compile-time path is that the schema must be written out
as types, and that the library's \code{ast_schema} vocabulary is
deliberately minimal: it records a tag and a list of child schemas, and
nothing else. It is not a grammar, it does not enforce arity at run time,
and it does not know which children are optional. A language whose nodes
have variable arity is still perfectly describable---the schema simply
lists the arity of one representative node---but the schema is a
description of the tag vocabulary first and a description of the shape
second.

\begin{notebox}[There is no \code{is_exhaustive}]
It is tempting to look for a trait that answers ``does this visitor
handle every tag of this schema?''. There is no such public function. On
the compile-time path, the question is answered by the
\code{static_assert} inside \code{compile_visitor}, and a user who wants
to ask it independently can call
\code{visitor_detail::covered<Schema, Cases...>()} directly. On the
runtime path, the question is answered by the constructor, which throws.
Both mechanisms are exhaustive by construction: there is no way to
construct a visitor that is missing a handler for a schema tag without
the program failing, at compile time or at run time.
\end{notebox}

\section{A worked example}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=Compile-time and runtime visitors over the same tree.,label=lst:visitor-example]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

using namespace dsl;

// ---- compile-time schema --------------------------------------------------
constexpr auto numSchema = ast_schema<"num"> ();
constexpr auto addSchema = ast_schema<"add"> (numSchema, numSchema);

void
handleNum (ASTNode &)
{
}
void
handleAdd (ASTNode &)
{
}

using NumCase = decltype (on<"num"> (&handleNum));
using AddCase = decltype (on<"add"> (&handleAdd));

static_assert (visitor_detail::covered<decltype (addSchema), NumCase, AddCase> (),
                "the add schema is covered by the num and add cases");
static_assert (!visitor_detail::covered<decltype (addSchema), NumCase> (),
                "the num case alone does not cover the add schema");

int
main ()
{
  // ---- compile-time path ------------------------------------------------
  auto visitor = compile_visitor (addSchema,
                                  on<"add"> ([] (ASTNode &n) { std::cout << "  add -> " << n.size () << " children\n"; }),
                                  on<"num"> ([] (ASTNode &n) { std::cout << "  num -> " << n.value () << "\n"; }));

  ASTNode tree ("add", { ASTNode ("num", "1"), ASTNode ("num", "2") });

  std::cout << "preorder:\n";
  visitor.walk (tree, VisitOrder::Preorder);
  std::cout << "postorder:\n";
  visitor.walk (tree, VisitOrder::Postorder);

  // A handler may also ask for the compile-time tag.
  auto tagged = compile_visitor (addSchema,
                                 on<"add"> ([] (ASTTag<"add"> tag, ASTNode &) { std::cout << "  handler for " << tag.value << "\n"; }),
                                 on<"num"> ([] (ASTNode &n) { std::cout << "  leaf " << n.value () << "\n"; }));
  tagged.visit (tree);

  // ---- runtime path -----------------------------------------------------
  ASTNode prototype ("add", { ASTNode ("num", "1"), ASTNode ("num", "2") });
  auto runtimeVisitor = compile_visitor (prototype,
                                         on<"add"> ([] (ASTNode &) { std::cout << "  r-add\n"; }),
                                         on<"num"> ([] (ASTNode &) { std::cout << "  r-num\n"; }));
  runtimeVisitor.walk (tree);

  // ---- the two failure modes -------------------------------------------
  try
    {
      ASTVisitor<AddCase> incomplete (prototype, on<"add"> (&handleAdd));
    }
  catch (const std::exception &ex)
    {
      std::cout << "missing case: " << ex.what () << "\n";
    }

  try
    {
      ASTNode foreign ("mul", { ASTNode ("num", "1"), ASTNode ("num", "2") });
      visitor.visit (foreign);
    }
  catch (const std::exception &ex)
    {
      std::cout << "foreign tag: " << ex.what () << "\n";
    }
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption=Output of \lstref{lst:visitor-example}.]
preorder:
  add -> 2 children
  num -> 1
  num -> 2
postorder:
  num -> 1
  num -> 2
  add -> 2 children
  handler for add
  r-add
  r-num
  r-num
missing case: visitor lacks a handler for num
foreign tag: node tag is outside visitor schema
\end{lstlisting}

The two failure modes at the end of the program are the two ways a visitor
can be wrong, and they are different in kind. The first is a visitor that
was built from a schema it does not fully handle; on the runtime path
this is a \code{std::invalid_argument} thrown from the constructor, and
on the compile-time path the same program would not have compiled at all.
The second is a node whose tag the schema never mentioned; this is always
a run-time error, because the node comes from data rather than from the
type system, and no static check can see it.

\section{Visitors in the wider toolkit}

A visitor is rarely the only thing a language needs, and it is worth
placing it among the other facilities of the toolkit, because the
combination is where the design pays off.

The rewrite feature of \chapref{ch:rewrite} and the e-graph of
\chapref{ch:egraphs} both operate on trees, and both are naturally
followed by a semantic pass. A bottom-up rewriter of
\chapref{ch:rewrite-sets} produces one tree; a visitor interprets it. An
equality-saturated pipeline produces a set of equivalent trees and
extracts one; a visitor interprets that one, and the \code{when_equal}
observer of the e-graph is the natural place to trigger it. The visitor
is the component that turns a tree into a result, and it is the
component that most often needs to be exhaustive, because a semantic pass
that silently ignores one kind of node produces wrong answers rather
than errors.

The compile-time schema is therefore not a curiosity. For a language
whose semantic passes are written by several people over several months,
the guarantee that every pass handles every kind of node is worth
having at the price of writing the schema down.

The PEG subsystem of \chapref{ch:peg-semantic-values} produces trees
whose tags are grammar nonterminals and whose leaves are tokens. Those
tags are not known when the grammar is written, and they are not a
closed set that a user enumerates by hand; a grammar may contain
nonterminals that no pass cares about. This is precisely the situation
in which the \emph{runtime} schema is the better choice: the prototype
is the tree that the grammar produced, and the visitor's constructor
checks coverage against it at the moment the visitor is built. The
library's own example
(\texttt{examples/26-equality-saturation.cpp}) uses exactly this
arrangement, with a one-tag schema derived from a leaf.

The two schema kinds are thus not competing designs but two ends of a
spectrum. The compile-time schema is right when the vocabulary is fixed
and known in advance; the runtime schema is right when the vocabulary is
discovered. A program that must handle both---a fixed core language plus
an extensible set of plug-in node kinds---can use the runtime path for
the extensible part and accept that the check happens when the visitor is
constructed rather than when it is compiled.

\section{Cost and the open/closed trade-off}

The dispatch is a linear scan. \code{visit} applies a fold over the tuple
of cases and compares the node's tag with each case's tag in turn, so a
visitor with \code{N} cases costs \code{O(N)} tag comparisons per node,
and \code{O(N)} string comparisons in the worst case, since the tags are
\code{std::string_view}s rather than integers. There is also a
\code{std::find} over the schema's tag list to validate the node's tag,
which is another linear scan. A visitor over a tree of \code{V} nodes
therefore costs \code{O(V * N)} tag comparisons.

For a language with a handful of node kinds this is irrelevant. It
becomes noticeable only for a language with dozens of tags visited over
millions of nodes, and even then the constant factor is small, because
the comparisons are between short string views. There is a second,
smaller cost that is worth mentioning: the \code{std::find} that checks
the node's tag against the schema's tag list is also linear, and it runs
once per node in addition to the case scan. A visitor over a tree of
\code{V} nodes therefore pays roughly \code{2V} linear scans in total,
which for a schema of a dozen tags and a tree of a million nodes is a
few tens of millions of string comparisons---measurable, but not usually
the bottleneck. The alternative---a \code{switch} on an enumeration---is
genuinely faster, and the library does not offer it, for a reason worth
stating: the tag vocabulary in DSLtk is a set of strings, and the whole
point of the schema mechanism is that the vocabulary is described as
data rather than as an enumeration in the source. A user who wants the
\code{switch} can have it by writing their own dispatch over their own
enumeration, and the visitor is convenient precisely because it does not
require one.

There is also a cost in the number of instantiations. Each distinct
visitor is a distinct \code{ASTVisitor} type, and each distinct
combination of cases produces a new instantiation of the dispatch fold.
A program that constructs visitors in a loop with a runtime-varying
number of cases will therefore generate code slowly; a program that
builds a fixed set of visitors at start-up will not notice. This is the
usual price of a template-based approach, and it is worth remembering
when the visitor is used as a general-purpose dispatch rather than as a
compile-time dispatch table.

The open/closed trade-off is the classic one, and DSLtk resolves it in
favour of \emph{closed}. A visitor is closed over the set of tags it was
given. Adding a new tag to a schema without adding a matching case is
therefore an error: a compilation failure on the compile-time path, and a
\code{std::invalid_argument} thrown from the constructor on the runtime
path. The alternative---an open visitor with a default case---would let
the program run and silently do nothing for the new tag, which is a
worse failure, because a language that grows a node kind usually grows it
in a way that \emph{some} semantic pass must handle.

The price of closure is that a visitor cannot be reused across two
languages with different tag sets, and that a pass that genuinely does
not care about some tag must still write a case for it. The library
offers no default case and no way to opt out of the requirement. For the
common case---a semantic pass over one language---this is exactly right,
and a user who needs an open visitor can write one in twenty lines, using
\code{node.tag ()} directly.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The schema is a tag vocabulary, not a grammar.} Its
  arities and its leaf values are ignored by the visitor. The
  compile-time schema records arities, but the check it supports is
  about tag coverage, not about arity.
\item \textbf{Exactly one case fires.} If two cases carry the same tag,
  the one registered first wins and the other is never called. There is
  no diagnostic for this.
\item \textbf{Handlers may mutate the tree.} A handler receives a
  mutable reference and may change a node's children. The traversal
  iterates over the live child vector rather than over a snapshot, so a
  handler that changes the children of a node that is currently being
  traversed invalidates the traversal's iterators. In preorder the
  handler for a node runs before that node's children are visited, so
  replacing a node's children is well defined; in postorder the handler
  for a node runs after its children have been visited, so the same
  replacement is safe. Mutating an \emph{ancestor's} child list from a
  descendant's handler is not safe in either order.
\item \textbf{The node type is deduced, so const trees are possible.} The
  visitor is generic over the node type, and a \code{const ASTNode} is
  visited successfully provided the handlers themselves accept a
  \code{const ASTNode &}. A handler that requires a mutable node cannot
  be applied to a const tree, and the failure is a compile error in the
  handler's own instantiation rather than a diagnostic from the
  visitor.
\item \textbf{The tag is a string.} Dispatch compares string views, so
  the cost of a comparison depends on the length of the common prefix,
  and two tags that differ only in case are different tags.
\item \textbf{The compile-time check is about coverage only.} A schema
  whose arities do not match the trees it will visit still compiles; the
  visitor does not check arity at run time either.
\item \textbf{\code{compile_visitor} needs the right overload.} A
  schema type selects the checked overload; an \code{ASTNode} selects
  the unchecked one. Passing something that is neither produces a
  confusing error from deep inside the \code{covered} predicate rather
  than a helpful one.
\item \textbf{No visitors over non-trees.} The traversal is defined in
  terms of \code{tag ()} and \code{children ()}, so a node type that
  offers those two members can be visited, but the visitor's own
  constructor insists on an \code{ASTNode} schema.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The visitor subsystem is a small piece of machinery with a large
ambition: to let a language's semantic passes be written as a list of
per-tag handlers, and to let the compiler check that the list is
complete. The two schema kinds serve the two situations in which that
check is available, and they share the same runtime visitor, the same
traversal, and the same handler shapes.

For the equality-saturated pipelines of \chapref{ch:egraphs}, a visitor
is the natural downstream consumer: the e-graph produces a representative
tree, and a visitor interprets it. The next chapters move on to the
remaining corners of the toolkit---binary streams, grammars, and the
preprocessor.
